Over the&nbsp;next 45 minutes,&nbsp;Mincieli&nbsp;took&nbsp;the students, who are part of&nbsp;Sony Music’s Global Scholars Program, through three versions of Keys’ “Empire State of Mind”&nbsp;and&nbsp;her contributions&nbsp;on each to illustrate&nbsp;how her career path has taken her from “producer to&nbsp;painting the sonic palette with the artist.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

“It’s incredible to see the growth in career paths available in the studio,” Mincieli, who also owns New York&#8217;s Jungle City studios,&nbsp;told the students.&nbsp;“The lines get blurred sometimes, so it’s important to learn the roles to put yourself in the best position and&nbsp;so you can&nbsp;advocate for&nbsp;[yourself].”&nbsp;&nbsp;

The students&nbsp;are part of&nbsp;a group&nbsp;of&nbsp;55 mostly college sophomores&nbsp;from 18 countries&nbsp;who received Sony Music scholarships&nbsp;to pursue degrees and careers in the recording industry&nbsp;—&nbsp;both on the creative and business side.&nbsp;The program sponsors aspiring music professionals with scholarships of $3,000 to $20,000 per academic year,&nbsp;renewable&nbsp;for up to&nbsp;three&nbsp;additional&nbsp;years, before they start college. A novelty in the music industry, the program is&nbsp;modeled off a scholarship program run by&nbsp;the Magic Johnson Foundation&nbsp;and resembles&nbsp;recruitment programs&nbsp;popular in the financial and legal industries.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

“It’s important for us to invest in the next generation of music professionals, and we want that investment to start early&nbsp;in&nbsp;their college career,”&nbsp;said&nbsp; Towalame&nbsp;Austin , executive&nbsp;vp&nbsp;in charge of Sony Music’s global philanthropy, environment and social impact,&nbsp;who oversees the program. “We&#8217;re committed to them&nbsp;for&nbsp;an&nbsp;average&nbsp;of&nbsp;four years.”&nbsp;

Funding is provided&nbsp;by Sony Music’s social justice fund,&nbsp;and&nbsp;part of the program involved covering&nbsp;flights and hotels for&nbsp;41 of the 55 students to&nbsp;come to&nbsp;New York for a week&nbsp;this summer&nbsp;to&nbsp;meet&nbsp;staff from 26 different Sony divisions, including&nbsp;CEO Rob&nbsp;Stringer ,&nbsp;studio legends like&nbsp;Mincieli,&nbsp;recording artists&nbsp;Miles Caton&nbsp;and Rapsody, label presidents, Orchard executives and business and legal affairs staff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Anthony Marshall, a sophomore at Coastal Carolina University&nbsp;in South Carolina,&nbsp;said&nbsp;the program introduced him to&nbsp;the breadth of the music&nbsp;careers available. Majoring in&nbsp;general music studies&nbsp;and minoring in psychology, Marshall was originally pursuing a career in music therapy before&nbsp;school&nbsp;and a job at his hometown orchestra introduced him to backstage and production jobs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

