“I’m just hoping to look for what I fit best,” Marshall&nbsp;said. “What Sony has taught me is connections are very&nbsp;valuable, and that has made me realize that I only got my jobs back home because of connections, which has made me appreciate this more.”&nbsp;

Recent U.S. college graduates face a challenging job market, with 5.6% of&nbsp;grads&nbsp;aged 22 to 27&nbsp;reporting being&nbsp;unemployed compared to&nbsp;a&nbsp;3.1% unemployment rate among all college&nbsp;grads, according to the most recent U.S.&nbsp;Bureau of Labor Statistics&nbsp;data.&nbsp;

A post on a music industry Reddit forum by someone&nbsp;who&nbsp;said&nbsp;they&nbsp;graduated in&nbsp;May 2024,&nbsp;titled “Job Hunting Is Hell,”&nbsp;recently&nbsp;attracted dozens of comments, including&nbsp;some&nbsp;suggesting the best&nbsp;way&nbsp;to find a job in the music biz is to create one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Royal Holloway&nbsp;University of London&nbsp;student&nbsp;Bradley Bolton said&nbsp;the Sony program helped him land an internship&nbsp;at the&nbsp;London-based&nbsp;Caribbean and Black music&nbsp;publisher&nbsp;Rekodi&nbsp;Music,&nbsp;which has given him the confidence to&nbsp;pursue a&nbsp;career in artist discovery and management.&nbsp;

“Hearing from the CEO of Sony Music and an artist today, it&#8217;s all about being yourself and trying to find your own path rather than following the next person,”&nbsp;said&nbsp;Bolton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Students like Bolton and Marshall are part of the second&nbsp;class&nbsp;of Sony Music’s Global Scholars program.&nbsp;The first group of scholars, who had a similar week-long program in New York two years ago,&nbsp;graduated this spring, and&nbsp;Austin&nbsp;said&nbsp;they are exploring ways to create an alumni group for the 110&nbsp;students&nbsp;in total.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

The week-long program&nbsp;helped&nbsp;Selena Lemmon, a legal studies and entertainment business studies major at&nbsp;Belmont&nbsp;University,&nbsp;make&nbsp;connections&nbsp;and find&nbsp;camaraderie, which&nbsp;has energized her&nbsp;for the&nbsp;hard work&nbsp;required&nbsp;to succeed in music, she&nbsp;said.&nbsp;&nbsp;

“When I was little, my mom told me that other people&#8217;s success never takes away from your own,” Lemmon&nbsp;said. “So&nbsp;until it&#8217;s your turn, learn to clap for others, which is&nbsp;something I really took to heart,&nbsp;especially in the music industry.”&nbsp;

Lemmon&nbsp;said&nbsp;she&nbsp;took&nbsp;the inspiration and&nbsp;lessons from&nbsp;a week in&nbsp;New York back to Nashville where she will “keep trucking through down there.”&nbsp;
